<xml>
  <block type="device_stepper_driver" x="25" y="25">
    <mutation uid="wheel62" />
    <field name="NAME">wheel</field>
    <field name="STEP">3</field>
    <field name="DIR">4</field>
    <field name="SPEED">600</field>
    <field name="DRIVE_MODE">RAMPED</field>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Purpose: A potentiometer controls continuous speed and direction, with acceleration 200 steps/s². This portable LOOP engine needs run on every pass. For fast STEP/DIR on ATmega168/328 use example 70 and STEP D9/D10. Motor supply and driver wiring must match the module.</comment>
    <field name="ACCEL">200</field>
    <field name="PULSE_US">5</field>
    <field name="ENGINE">LOOP</field>
  </block>
  <block type="arduino_loop_section" x="450" y="25">
    <statement name="DO">
      <block type="controls_if">
        <value name="IF0">
          <block type="logic_compare">
            <field name="OP">GTE</field>
            <value name="A">
              <block type="math_arithmetic">
                <field name="OP">MINUS</field>
                <value name="A">
                  <block type="arduino_millis" />
                </value>
                <value name="B">
                  <block type="variables_get">
                    <field name="VAR">lastRead</field>
                  </block>
                </value>
              </block>
            </value>
            <value name="B">
              <block type="math_number">
                <field name="NUM">20</field>
              </block>
            </value>
          </block>
        </value>
        <comment pinned="false" w="380" h="190">Read and map the potentiometer only every 20 ms. ADC conversion and arithmetic on every pass would delay the software STEP pulses. Unsigned millis subtraction also works across rollover.</comment>
        <statement name="DO0">
          <block type="variables_set">
            <field name="VAR">lastRead</field>
            <value name="VALUE">
              <block type="arduino_millis" />
            </value>
            <next>
              <block type="device_stepper_speed">
                <field name="DEVICE">wheel62</field>
                <value name="SPEED">
                  <block type="base_map">
                    <value name="NUM">
                      <block type="inout_analog_read">
                        <field name="PIN">A0</field>
                        <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Measure the voltage on this analog input. On ATmega168/328, the ADC returns 0–1023; other boards may use a different ADC range. A potentiometer wiper or analog sensor connects here.</comment>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="FROM_LOW">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">0</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="FROM_HIGH">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">1023</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="TO_LOW">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">-600</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <value name="DMAX">
                      <block type="math_number">
                        <field name="NUM">600</field>
                      </block>
                    </value>
                    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="160">Convert a value from one numeric range to another. All five inputs can be expressions. Arduino map uses integer arithmetic and does not clamp values outside the source range.</comment>
                  </block>
                </value>
              </block>
            </next>
          </block>
        </statement>
        <next>
          <block type="device_stepper_run">
            <field name="DEVICE">wheel62</field>
            <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Service the motor on every loop pass. This emits a step only when due and returns quickly. Long delays would interrupt smooth movement.</comment>
          </block>
        </next>
      </block>
    </statement>
    <comment pinned="false" w="340" h="170">Repeats until power is removed. Each block runs in order, then execution returns to the top. Keep this loop responsive when servicing motors, music or serial input.</comment>
  </block>
  <block type="arduino_typed_variable" x="25" y="370">
    <field name="VAR">lastRead</field>
    <field name="TYPE">uint32_t</field>
    <field name="QUALIFIER">normal</field>
    <value name="VALUE">
      <block type="math_number">
        <field name="NUM">0</field>
      </block>
    </value>
    <comment pinned="false" w="380" h="190">Remember when the control was last sampled; the variable is global so it persists between loop passes.</comment>
  </block>
</xml>
